#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The existing-test audit's tool (a), the deletion test (docs/TEST_AUDIT.md): for each test, every
product function the coverage nightly measured it to run is replaced by a stub raising
`NotImplementedError("deletion audit")`, in a throwaway copy, and the test is run alone. Every test
that still passes is listed. It lists suspects only: it never says whether a finding is real.

    scripts/nmas-test-audit-deletion --reach DIR (--commit SHA | --then DIR) [--root DIR]
        [--scope PATH ...] [--python PY] [--jobs N] [--offset N] [--limit N] [--baseline]
        [--timeout S] [--stub-harness] [--plan-only] [--workdir DIR] [--out FILE]

**The reach map.** `--reach` is the coverage nightly's `coverage-drilldown` artifact, unpacked
(its `coverage.json`), read by `scripts/nmas_mutation.py`'s `load_reach`: `{path::qualname: [test
id]}`, each function's tests those whose per-test contexts ran a line of its body, any phase
(setup and teardown too), in the test's own process. It is inverted to test → functions.

**The scope.** `--scope` names modules (a path, or a folder ending in `/`); the tests run are those
the map says reach a function in one of them (the default: the operator's named modules,
DEFAULT_SCOPE). Every function such a test reaches is stubbed, in scope or not: the deletion test
asks whether the test needs any of the code it runs.

**The harness.** A function the map says EVERY test in it reaches is reached by the run's own
machinery, never by one test's subject (in run #36's map: `redact.guard_new_handlers`, whose
`addHandler` closure pytest's logging plugin calls for every test, and the
`install_log_redaction` it calls). It is not stubbed, it does not put a test in scope, and it
is named in the output; `--stub-harness` stubs it too. Measured on run #36's map: with it, every
one of the map's 20,879 tests is in scope (redact.py is), and a test reaching no product code
of its own is listed as still passing; without it, 11,493.

**Staleness.** The map is one commit's. Each function's text hash (`nmas_mutation.function_hash`:
its own lines) is taken from the map's commit (`--commit`, read by `git show` in `--root`) or from
the sources as the map measured them (`--then DIR`, the same paths under it), and from `--root`
now. A function whose hash moved, or that either side lacks, is stale; a test reaching a stale
function is `stale` and is not run (its stub set is not known: a stale function is never stubbed
blind).

**The stubs.** Each module is parsed (`ast`); a function's body (after its docstring, which is
kept) is replaced by the stub at the same byte position (from its first statement's first
decorator, if it has one), so its signature, decorators and the rest of the file stay as they
are; a method by its qualname. Two stubs run in turn (`--stubs`, STUBS): `raise` (`raise
NotImplementedError("deletion audit")`, the operator's) and, when the test did not pass under
it, `none` (the body deleted: `return None`). The raising stub fails any test that calls the
function directly, asserting or not, so on its own it can never list a test that asserts
nothing (the canary's first run); a test passing under either stub is listed, the detail naming
which. A qualname defined more than once in its module (two arms of an `if`), or found nowhere,
or a stubbed module that does not compile or does not hold exactly one more stub body per
function chosen, makes the test `not stubbable`, and it is not run. A generator so stubbed is a
plain function, raising (or giving None) on call rather than on its first item.

**The copy and the run.** `--root`'s files as `tests.source_index.tracked()` lists them are
copied into one throwaway folder per job (`--jobs`, default 2), outside `--root`, removed at the
end. Each test, in an order fixed by the sha256 of its id (`--offset`, `--limit` take a shard of
it), has its stubbed modules written into its job's copy, is run alone as `<python> -m pytest -q
-p no:cacheprovider --tb=line -rfEs <id>` there (no bytecode, `NMAS_COPY_OF=<root>`, so a scan
through `tracked()` in the copy reads `--root`'s index), and the copy's files are put back. A
test id the map names that the copy's own collection (`--collect-only`, of the test files the
copy holds: pytest given a missing path collects nothing at all) does not list is `not
collected`, its file gone or not, and is not run. `--baseline` first runs each test unstubbed in
the copy; one that does not pass so is `fails unstubbed`, its stubbed runs not made.

**Results:** `still passes` (passed, or xpassed, under a stub), `fails` (failed or errored
under every stub run; the detail says in which phase, and whether the raising stub's message
was reached), `skipped` (skipped or xfailed: no verdict), `no outcome` (pytest reported no test
outcome: its exit code and last line are given), `timed out` (past `--timeout`, default 300 s:
the suite's own per-process bound, scripts/nmas-test), `fails unstubbed`, `stale`, `not
collected`, `not stubbable`.

**Output:** a TSV `test_id<TAB>modules<TAB>functions stubbed<TAB>result<TAB>detail`: the modules
are the test's in-scope modules it reaches; the functions are all of them for a `still passes`
row and the in-scope ones with a count of the rest for every other row; the detail holds the
test's tier (the map's tier records), pytest's own outcome words and the run's seconds. Then `#`
lines: the scope, the counts by result, the harness, the stale functions, the timing, and any
tool error by test. Exit 0; 1 when the copy's collection did not run or a test hit a tool
error; 2 for a bad argument.
"""
import argparse
import ast
import collections
import functools
import hashlib
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import os
import queue
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
REPO = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
sys.path.insert(0, REPO)
# Phase 4 section 8.1: the interpreter flask-app runs, before any other import.
from modules.app_interpreter import adopt  # noqa: E402
adopt(__name__)


def _load(name, path):
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(name, path)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


nm = _load("nmas_mutation", os.path.join(REPO, "scripts", "nmas_mutation.py"))
pydefs = nm.pydefs
# `function_hash` parses its source once per function asked: thousands of functions in a few
# hundred modules took 2 minutes. One parse per source text, in this process's own copy of
# pydefs (the lists it returns are only read).
pydefs.definitions = functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)(pydefs.definitions)

#: The operator's named modules (docs/TEST_AUDIT.md, tool (b)'s full scope): deploy; credentials
#: and secrets; drift; Needs attention; SDN detect and respond; egress; every Phase 8 module.
DEFAULT_SCOPE = (
    "modules/nsot/deploy.py", "modules/pipeline.py", "modules/deploy_job.py",
    "modules/credentials.py", "modules/secrets_store.py", "modules/nsot/credential_rotation.py",
    "modules/redact.py", "modules/drift_check.py", "modules/attention.py",
    "modules/ai/", "modules/sdn/")

STUB = 'raise NotImplementedError("deletion audit")'
STUB_MESSAGE = "deletion audit"
#: The two stubs, run in this order (`--stubs`): the operator's, which raises, and the body
#: deleted, which returns None. The raising stub fails a test that calls the function directly
#: and asserts nothing about what it gave, so on its own it can never list one (the canary's
#: first run, 2026-11-03); the deleted body lets that test pass, and fails one that reads the
#: result. A test passing under either is listed, naming the stub.
STUBS = {"raise": STUB, "none": "return None"}
RESULTS = ("still passes", "fails", "skipped", "no outcome", "timed out", "fails unstubbed",
           "stale", "not collected", "not stubbable")
#: The suite's own per-process bound (scripts/nmas-test, NMAS_TEST_TIMEOUT's default).
TIMEOUT_S = 300
#: The copy's collection: measured about 40 s for the whole suite (tool (d), 2026-11-03); the
#: bound is about 2.5 times a loaded run of it.
COLLECT_TIMEOUT_S = 240
#: Variables that would carry the calling run's pytest or coverage configuration into a child.
_DROP_ENV = ("PYTEST_ADDOPTS", "PYTEST_CURRENT_TEST", "PYTEST_XDIST_WORKER",
             "PYTEST_XDIST_WORKER_COUNT", "PYTEST_XDIST_TESTRUNUID", "PYTEST_PLUGINS")
OUTCOME_RE = re.compile(r"(\d+) (passed|failed|errors?|skipped|xfailed|xpassed|deselected)")


# --- the map --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def in_scope(path, scope):
    return any(path == s or (s.endswith("/") and path.startswith(s)) for s in scope)


def by_test(functions):
    """`{test id: {path::qualname}}` from the map's `{path::qualname: [test id]}`."""
    out = collections.defaultdict(set)
    for k, tests in functions.items():
        for t in tests:
            out[t].add(k)
    return dict(out)


def harness(functions, tests):
    """The functions every test in the map reaches (see the module's docstring)."""
    n = len(tests)
    return sorted(k for k, t in functions.items() if n and len(set(t)) == n)


def order(tests):
    """A fixed order no file or class groups: the sha256 of each id."""
    return sorted(tests, key=lambda t: hashlib.sha256(t.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest())


# --- staleness ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def reader_then(root, commit=None, then=None):
    """A function giving a path's text as the map measured it, or None where it held none."""
    cache = {}

    def read(path):
        if path not in cache:
            if then:
                full = os.path.join(then, *path.split("/"))
                cache[path] = (open(full, encoding="utf-8").read()
                               if os.path.isfile(full) else None)
            else:
                # git pinned to the checkout it reads (C931's one runner), never climbing above.
                from modules.nsot.repo import run_git
                r = run_git(root, "show", f"{commit}:{path}", timeout=30)
                cache[path] = r.stdout if r.returncode == 0 else None
        return cache[path]
    return read


def read_now(root, path):
    full = os.path.join(root, *path.split("/"))
    if not os.path.isfile(full):
        return None
    with open(full, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        return fh.read()


def stale_functions(keys, root, read_then):
    """`{key: why}` for each function whose text moved since the map, or that a side lacks."""
    now_cache, out = {}, {}
    for k in sorted(keys):
        path, qualname = k.split("::", 1)
        if path not in now_cache:
            now_cache[path] = read_now(root, path)
        then = nm.function_hash(read_then(path), qualname)
        now = nm.function_hash(now_cache[path], qualname)
        if not now:
            out[k] = "not defined now"
        elif not then:
            out[k] = "not defined in the map's sources"
        elif then != now:
            out[k] = f"its text moved ({then[:12]} in the map, {now[:12]} now)"
    return out


# --- the stub -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def _offsets(data):
    """Byte offset of each line's start (1-based line numbers; ast's columns are UTF-8 bytes)."""
    starts, pos = [0, 0], 0
    for line in data.splitlines(keepends=True):
        pos += len(line)
        starts.append(pos)
    return starts


def _is_docstring(stmt):
    return (isinstance(stmt, ast.Expr) and isinstance(stmt.value, ast.Constant)
            and isinstance(stmt.value.value, str))


def body_spans(source):
    """`{qualname: [(start, end)]}`: the byte span of each counted function's body after its
    docstring, as pydefs finds the definitions (one walker, scripts/pydefs.py)."""
    data = source.encode("utf-8")
    starts = _offsets(data)
    nodes = {(n.lineno, n.name): n for n in ast.walk(ast.parse(source))
             if isinstance(n, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef))}
    out = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for d in pydefs.definitions(source):
        if d.kind == "class":
            continue
        node = nodes.get((d.lineno, d.name))
        if node is None:
            continue
        body = node.body[1:] if (_is_docstring(node.body[0]) and len(node.body) > 1) \
            else node.body
        first, last = body[0], body[-1]
        # A decorated statement's lineno is its `def` or `class` line: its `@` lines come first,
        # each at the statement's own column.
        line = min([first.lineno] + [x.lineno for x in getattr(first, "decorator_list", [])])
        out[d.qualname].append((starts[line] + first.col_offset,
                                starts[last.end_lineno] + last.end_col_offset))
    return dict(out)


def stub_source(source, qualnames, spans=None, stub=STUB):
    """`(new source or None, {qualname: why not})`: *source* with each named function's body
    replaced by *stub*. A qualname found nowhere, or defined more than once, is refused by name;
    a result that does not compile refuses every name."""
    spans = body_spans(source) if spans is None else spans
    refused, chosen = {}, []
    for q in sorted(qualnames):
        found = spans.get(q, [])
        if not found:
            refused[q] = "no counted definition of it in the module"
        elif len(found) > 1:
            refused[q] = f"defined {len(found)} times in the module"
        else:
            chosen.append(found[0])
    if refused:
        return None, refused
    data = source.encode("utf-8")
    for start, end in sorted(chosen, reverse=True):
        data = data[:start] + stub.encode("utf-8") + data[end:]
    new = data.decode("utf-8")
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(new)
        compile(tree, "<stubbed>", "exec", dont_inherit=True)
    except SyntaxError as e:
        return None, {q: f"the stubbed module does not compile: {e.msg}, line {e.lineno}"
                      for q in qualnames}
    # It compiling is not enough: exactly the chosen functions more must now be the stub.
    if stub_bodies(tree, stub) - _stub_bodies_of(source, stub) != len(chosen):
        return None, {q: "the stubbed module does not hold exactly one stub per function"
                      for q in qualnames}
    return new, {}


@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=1024)
def _stub_bodies_of(source, stub):
    """`stub_bodies` of an original module (a few hundred texts, each asked many times)."""
    return stub_bodies(ast.parse(source), stub)


def stub_bodies(tree, stub):
    """How many functions in *tree* have *stub* as their whole body after a docstring."""
    want = ast.dump(ast.parse(stub).body[0])
    n = 0
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)):
            body = node.body[1:] if (_is_docstring(node.body[0]) and len(node.body) > 1) \
                else node.body
            n += len(body) == 1 and ast.dump(body[0]) == want
    return n


# --- the copy and the run -------------------------------------------------------------------

def tracked_files(root):
    """`--root`'s files as its git index lists them (tests/source_index.py, C585)."""
    from tests.source_index import tracked
    return tracked(root=root)


def make_copy(root, files, dest):
    for src in files:
        dst = os.path.join(dest, os.path.relpath(src, root))
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dst), exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(src, dst)
    return dest


def child_env(root):
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items()
           if k not in _DROP_ENV and not k.startswith("COV_CORE_")}
    env["PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE"] = "1"
    env["NMAS_COPY_OF"] = os.path.abspath(root)
    return env


def collect(copy, python, files, env):
    """The ids pytest collects in *copy* for *files*, or None when the collection did not run."""
    cmd = [python, "-m", "pytest", "--collect-only", "-q", "-p", "no:cacheprovider",
           "--continue-on-collection-errors", *files]
    r = subprocess.run(cmd, cwd=copy, env=env, capture_output=True, text=True,
                       timeout=COLLECT_TIMEOUT_S)
    text = r.stdout + r.stderr
    if (not re.search(r"\d+ tests? collected|no tests collected|collected \d+ items?", text)
            or "ERROR: file or directory not found" in text):
        return None
    return {line.strip() for line in r.stdout.splitlines() if "::" in line and
            not line.startswith(("ERROR", "FAILED"))}


def run_one(copy, python, test_id, env, timeout):
    """`{"outcome": passed|failed|error|skipped|xfailed|xpassed|none, "words", "phase",
    "reached", "seconds"}` for *test_id* run alone in *copy*."""
    cmd = [python, "-m", "pytest", "-q", "-p", "no:cacheprovider", "--tb=line", "-rfEs",
           test_id]
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(cmd, cwd=copy, env=env, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return {"outcome": "timeout", "words": f"past {timeout} s", "phase": "",
                "reached": False, "seconds": time.monotonic() - t0}
    out = r.stdout + r.stderr
    counts = collections.Counter()
    for line in out.splitlines()[-5:]:
        for n, word in OUTCOME_RE.findall(line):
            counts["error" if word.startswith("error") else word] += int(n)
    if counts["failed"]:
        outcome = "failed"
    elif counts["error"]:
        outcome = "error"
    elif counts["passed"] or counts["xpassed"]:
        outcome = "xpassed" if counts["xpassed"] and not counts["passed"] else "passed"
    elif counts["xfailed"]:
        outcome = "xfailed"
    elif counts["skipped"]:
        outcome = "skipped"
    else:
        outcome = "none"
    phase = ""
    m = re.search(r"ERROR at (setup|teardown) of", out)
    if m:
        phase = m.group(1)
    elif re.search(r"ERROR collecting ", out):
        # The test's own file did not import: a stubbed function runs at its import.
        phase = "collection"
    elif outcome == "failed":
        phase = "call"
    words = ", ".join(f"{n} {w}" for w, n in sorted(counts.items()))
    if not words:
        last = [x for x in out.replace(copy, "<copy>").splitlines() if x.strip()][-1:] or [""]
        words = f"exit {r.returncode}: {' '.join(last[0].split())[:160]}"
    return {"outcome": outcome, "words": words, "phase": phase,
            "reached": STUB_MESSAGE in out, "seconds": time.monotonic() - t0}


def functions_column(result, funcs, scope):
    """Every function stubbed, for a listed test (what the reviewer reads); the counts in and
    outside the scope for any other row (the full sets are the map's; listing them for 11,000
    rows made a 15 MB file)."""
    if result == "still passes":
        return ", ".join(sorted(funcs))
    ins = sum(1 for f in funcs if in_scope(f.split("::", 1)[0], scope))
    return f"{ins} in the scope, {len(funcs) - ins} outside it"


# The output is committed: an address literal, an email and a home path in any column (a
# parametrize id can carry one) are replaced as tools (c) and (d) replace them, one home.
def _load_script(name, path):
    """A script without `.py`, loaded as a module (its `adopt` does nothing under a name that
    is not `__main__`)."""
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name, path)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(importlib.util.spec_from_loader(name, loader))
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


public = _load_script("nmas_test_audit_dead",
                      os.path.join(REPO, "scripts", "nmas-test-audit-dead")).public


def result_of(run):
    if run["outcome"] in ("passed", "xpassed"):
        return "still passes"
    if run["outcome"] in ("skipped", "xfailed"):
        return "skipped"
    if run["outcome"] == "timeout":
        return "timed out"
    if run["outcome"] == "none":
        return "no outcome"
    return "fails"


# --- the audit ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def plan(reach, root, scope, read_then, stub_harness=False):
    """Which tests run and which do not, before anything runs."""
    functions = reach["functions"]
    tests = by_test(functions)
    held = [] if stub_harness else harness(functions, tests)
    held_set = set(held)
    scoped = [t for t, fs in tests.items()
              if any(in_scope(f.split("::", 1)[0], scope) for f in fs - held_set)]
    stub_keys = set().union(*(tests[t] for t in scoped)) - held_set if scoped else set()
    stale = stale_functions(stub_keys, root, read_then)
    return {"tests": tests, "harness": held, "scoped": order(scoped), "stale": stale,
            "map_tests": len(tests), "map_functions": len(functions)}


def audit(root, reach_folder, commit=None, then=None, scope=DEFAULT_SCOPE, python=None,
          jobs=2, offset=0, limit=None, baseline=False, timeout=TIMEOUT_S,
          stub_harness=False, plan_only=False, workdir=None, progress=None,
          stubs=tuple(STUBS)):
    root = os.path.abspath(root)
    if not stubs or any(s not in STUBS for s in stubs):
        raise ValueError(f"stubs must be among {', '.join(STUBS)}: {stubs!r}")
    python = python or sys.executable
    reach = nm.load_reach(reach_folder)
    if reach is None:
        raise SystemExit(f"no coverage.json in {reach_folder}")
    tiers = reach.get("tiers") or {}
    p = plan(reach, root, scope, reader_then(root, commit, then), stub_harness)
    chosen = p["scoped"][offset:offset + limit if limit is not None else None]
    rows = []
    out = {"plan": p, "chosen": len(chosen), "rows": rows, "collected": None,
           "wall": 0.0, "run_seconds": [], "runs": 0, "jobs": jobs, "stubs": tuple(stubs),
           "errors": []}
    if plan_only:
        return out
    files = tracked_files(root)
    env = child_env(root)
    base = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="nmas-deletion-", dir=workdir)
    lock = threading.Lock()
    try:
        copies = [make_copy(root, files, os.path.join(base, f"copy-{i}")) for i in range(jobs)]
        # Only files the copy holds: pytest given one it cannot find collects nothing at all.
        test_files = sorted(f for f in {t.split("::", 1)[0] for t in chosen}
                            if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(copies[0], *f.split("/"))))
        collected = collect(copies[0], python, test_files, env) if test_files else set()
        if collected is None:
            out["collected"] = None
            return out
        out["collected"] = len(collected)
        spans_cache, source_cache = {}, {}

        def original(path):
            if path not in source_cache:
                source_cache[path] = read_now(copies[0], path)
            return source_cache[path]

        def spans(path):
            if path not in spans_cache:
                try:
                    spans_cache[path] = body_spans(original(path))
                except SyntaxError:
                    spans_cache[path] = {}
            return spans_cache[path]

        def row(t, result, detail, funcs):
            mods = sorted({f.split("::", 1)[0] for f in funcs if in_scope(f.split("::", 1)[0],
                                                                          scope)})
            shown = functions_column(result, funcs, scope)
            tier = (tiers.get(t) or {}).get("tier", "")
            detail = "; ".join(x for x in (f"tier {tier}" if tier else "", detail) if x)
            with lock:
                rows.append(tuple(public(x) for x in (t, ", ".join(mods), shown, result,
                                                      detail)))
                if progress:
                    progress(len(rows), len(chosen))

        # Every module's original text and spans, read before any job writes a stub: a job
        # writing its copy while another first reads one would take a stub for the original.
        for t in chosen:
            for f in p["tests"][t]:
                path = f.split("::", 1)[0]
                if original(path) is not None:
                    spans(path)
        work = queue.Queue()
        for t in chosen:
            funcs = p["tests"][t] - set(p["harness"])
            stale = sorted(f for f in funcs if f in p["stale"])
            if t not in collected:
                gone = t.split("::", 1)[0] not in test_files
                row(t, "not collected", "its file is not in the copy" if gone
                    else "the copy's collection does not list it", funcs)
            elif stale:
                # Why each moved is said once, in the `#` lines.
                row(t, "stale", "moved since the map: " + ", ".join(stale), funcs)
            else:
                work.put((t, funcs))

        def stubbed(funcs, stub):
            """`({path: stubbed text}, {key: why not})` for one test's functions."""
            by_path = collections.defaultdict(set)
            for f in funcs:
                path, q = f.split("::", 1)
                by_path[path].add(q)
            new, refused = {}, {}
            for path, qs in sorted(by_path.items()):
                src = original(path)
                if src is None:
                    refused.update({f"{path}::{q}": "the module is not in the copy" for q in qs})
                    continue
                text, why = stub_source(src, qs, spans(path), STUBS[stub])
                refused.update({f"{path}::{q}": w for q, w in why.items()})
                new[path] = text
            return new, refused

        def run_stubbed(copy, t, new):
            try:
                for path, text in new.items():
                    with open(os.path.join(copy, *path.split("/")), "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                        fh.write(text)
                return run_one(copy, python, t, env, timeout)
            finally:
                for path in new:
                    with open(os.path.join(copy, *path.split("/")), "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                        fh.write(original(path))

        def one(copy, t, funcs):
            new, refused = stubbed(funcs, stubs[0])
            if refused:
                row(t, "not stubbable",
                    "; ".join(f"{k}: {w}" for k, w in sorted(refused.items())), funcs)
                return
            if baseline:
                b = run_one(copy, python, t, env, timeout)
                if b["outcome"] not in ("passed", "xpassed"):
                    row(t, "fails unstubbed", f"unstubbed: {b['outcome']} ({b['words']})", funcs)
                    return
            parts, results, seconds = [], [], 0.0
            for i, stub in enumerate(stubs):
                if i:
                    new, _ = stubbed(funcs, stub)
                r = run_stubbed(copy, t, new)
                seconds += r["seconds"]
                part = f"{stub}: {r['outcome']} ({r['words']})"
                if r["phase"]:
                    part += f" in {r['phase']}"
                if stub == "raise" and r["outcome"] in ("failed", "error"):
                    part += (", the stub's NotImplementedError reached" if r["reached"]
                             else ", the stub's message not in its output")
                parts.append(part)
                results.append(result_of(r))
                if results[-1] == "still passes":
                    break
            with lock:
                out["run_seconds"].append(seconds)
                out["runs"] += len(results)
            if "still passes" in results:
                result = "still passes"
            elif "fails" in results:
                result = "fails"
            elif "skipped" in results:
                result = "skipped"
            elif "no outcome" in results:
                result = "no outcome"
            else:
                result = "timed out"
            files_n = len({f.split("::", 1)[0] for f in funcs})
            row(t, result, "; ".join(parts) + f"; {seconds:.1f} s; {len(funcs)} functions in "
                f"{files_n} modules stubbed", funcs)

        def worker(copy):
            while True:
                try:
                    t, funcs = work.get_nowait()
                except queue.Empty:
                    return
                try:
                    one(copy, t, funcs)
                except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - said in the output, and exit 1
                    with lock:
                        out["errors"].append((t, repr(e)))

        t0 = time.monotonic()
        threads = [threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(c,)) for c in copies]
        for th in threads:
            th.start()
        for th in threads:
            th.join()
        out["wall"] = time.monotonic() - t0
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(base, ignore_errors=True)
    rows.sort()
    return out


def report_lines(out, scope, commit):
    p = out["plan"]
    counts = collections.Counter(r[3] for r in out["rows"])
    secs = out["run_seconds"]
    lines = [f"# scope: {', '.join(scope)}",
             f"# map: {p['map_tests']} tests, {p['map_functions']} functions; its sources: "
             + (f"commit {commit}" if commit else "the folder given as --then"),
             f"# in scope: {len(p['scoped'])} tests; this run: {out['chosen']} "
             f"(the sha256 order, --offset and --limit); stubs in turn: "
             + ", ".join(f"{s} ({STUBS[s]})" for s in out["stubs"]),
             "# harness (every test in the map reaches it, not stubbed): "
             + (", ".join(p["harness"]) or "none"),
             f"# stale functions among the in-scope tests' functions: {len(p['stale'])}"]
    lines += [f"#   {k}: {w}" for k, w in sorted(p["stale"].items())]
    lines += ["# results: " + ", ".join(f"{r} {counts.get(r, 0)}" for r in RESULTS)]
    if secs:
        lines += [f"# tests run: {len(secs)}, pytest runs {out['runs']}, {sum(secs):.0f} s of "
                  f"runs, mean {sum(secs)/len(secs):.2f} s a test, wall {out['wall']:.0f} s at "
                  f"--jobs {out['jobs']}, {out['wall']/len(secs):.2f} s of wall a test"]
    lines += [f"# TOOL ERROR {t}: {e}" for t, e in out["errors"]]
    return lines


def main(argv=None):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="The test audit's deletion test, tool (a).")
    ap.add_argument("--reach", required=True, help="the coverage-drilldown folder")
    ap.add_argument("--root", default=REPO, help="the tree whose tests run (default: this "
                    "checkout)")
    src = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=True)
    src.add_argument("--commit", help="the map's commit, read in --root by git show")
    src.add_argument("--then", help="a folder holding the sources as the map measured them")
    ap.add_argument("--scope", nargs="+", default=list(DEFAULT_SCOPE))
    ap.add_argument("--python", default=sys.executable)
    ap.add_argument("--jobs", type=int, default=2)
    ap.add_argument("--offset", type=int, default=0)
    ap.add_argument("--limit", type=int)
    ap.add_argument("--baseline", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--timeout", type=float, default=TIMEOUT_S)
    ap.add_argument("--stub-harness", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--stubs", default=",".join(STUBS),
                    help=f"the stubs to run in turn, comma-separated (default {','.join(STUBS)})")
    ap.add_argument("--plan-only", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--workdir", help="where the copies are made (default: the system's "
                    "temporary folder)")
    ap.add_argument("--out", help="write the output here as well as to stdout")
    a = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if a.jobs < 1 or a.offset < 0 or (a.limit is not None and a.limit < 0):
        ap.error("--jobs must be at least 1, --offset and --limit not negative")
    stubs = tuple(s for s in a.stubs.split(",") if s)
    if not stubs or any(s not in STUBS for s in stubs):
        ap.error(f"--stubs takes {', '.join(STUBS)}: {a.stubs!r}")

    def progress(done, total):
        if done % 25 == 0 or done == total:
            print(f"nmas-test-audit-deletion: {done}/{total}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)

    out = audit(a.root, a.reach, a.commit, a.then, tuple(a.scope), a.python, a.jobs,
                a.offset, a.limit, a.baseline, a.timeout, a.stub_harness, a.plan_only,
                a.workdir, progress, stubs)
    if not a.plan_only and out["collected"] is None:
        print("nmas-test-audit-deletion: the copy's collection did not run", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    text = "\n".join(["test_id\tmodules\tfunctions stubbed\tresult\tdetail"]
                     + ["\t".join(r) for r in out["rows"]]
                     + report_lines(out, a.scope, a.commit)) + "\n"
    sys.stdout.write(text)
    if a.out:
        with open(a.out, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(text)
    return 1 if out["errors"] else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
